te`, `connection`, `calls[].state` / `ended` and reason keys may gain values without it changing, so treat unrecognized values neutrally (an unrecognized `serving.state` as not serving \u2014 this schema itself parses an unrecognized `serving.state` as `off`, `policy` as `interim`, `channel` as `bridge_only`)."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Session metadata the CLI emits at the start of each turn, normally ahead of every other message of that turn: session_id, model, working directory, tools, MCP servers, slash commands, permission mode, and the capabilities list for feature detection.")),z0=f(()=>u({type:R("stream_event"),event:u0(),parent_tool_use_id:o().nullable(),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),ttft_ms:v().int().optional(),thinking_display:o().optional().describe("@internal The thinking.display Claude Code put in the API request body for the response this event belongs to. If anything rewrites the request on its way to the API, this value still states what Claude Code sent, not what the API received. Two responses of one turn can differ. Set on `message_start`, on a thinking block's `content_block_start` and on `thinking_delta` events when the body had a display; absent on every other event, on Remote Control (interactive terminal) sessions and from older producers. An event that the API did not send as it stands can lack the key. The key states which display the text was requested with, and nothing about how to show it. An absent key states nothing: neither which display was requested, nor that none was."),abandoned_blocks:u({api_message_id:o().describe("The API's id (`msg_\u2026`) of the response that was cut short: `event.message.id` on its `message_start`, `message.id` on its assistant messages. Not a message `uuid`. Should two responses share an id, the one meant is the one this `message_stop` closes."),from_block_index:v().int().nonnegative().describe("Blocks of the response at this index and above have no assistant message and never will. It is an `event.i